WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.753
Quantization changes the approximations used during inference,

00:00:02.753 --> 00:00:06.921
so quality loss may be subtle rather than a complete failure.

00:00:06.921 --> 00:00:11.090
A response can remain fluent while becoming less precise, more repetitive,

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.743
or more likely to miss a constraint.

00:00:13.743 --> 00:00:17.153
Hallucination is influenced by the base model, prompt, data,

00:00:17.153 --> 00:00:19.427
and sampling as well as precision;

00:00:19.427 --> 00:00:22.838
quantization does not create or remove it in isolation,

00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:25.112
but aggressive compression can reduce reliability.

00:00:25.112 --> 00:00:28.901
Reasoning tasks may reveal mistakes in intermediate steps or consistency.

00:00:28.901 --> 00:00:32.691
Coding tasks can expose errors through compilation, tests, API use,

00:00:32.691 --> 00:00:34.207
or adherence to requirements.

00:00:34.207 --> 00:00:37.997
Instruction following should be evaluated with prompts containing several constraints.

00:00:37.997 --> 00:00:42.544
Long-context tests should check whether the model retrieves details from early and

00:00:42.544 --> 00:00:45.576
late portions rather than merely producing plausible prose.

00:00:45.576 --> 00:00:47.092
Quality sensitivity is workload-specific.

00:00:47.092 --> 00:00:52.397
A conversational assistant may tolerate a compact quantization that is unacceptable for structured extraction,

00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:54.292
technical analysis, or domain terminology.

00:00:54.292 --> 00:00:59.977
Different model architectures and quantization methods can react differently at the same nominal bit level.

00:00:59.977 --> 00:01:04.145
A strong smaller model may outperform a larger aggressively compressed model.

00:01:04.145 --> 00:01:08.314
Evaluate outputs with clear criteria: factual correctness, task completion, format compliance,

00:01:08.314 --> 00:01:09.830
consistency, and useful uncertainty.

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:15.135
Use deterministic or controlled settings where possible and repeat prompts because sampling introduces variation.

00:01:15.135 --> 00:01:19.304
Do not assume that a popular quant level is good enough,

00:01:19.304 --> 00:01:23.093
and do not assume every difference is caused by quantization.

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:28.020
Compare against a higher-precision reference and the real acceptance threshold for the task.
